public static class GlobalDosDevices { public static bool HoldsDriveLetter(char letter) }. It returns false for anything that is not an ASCII letter. Otherwise it upper-cases the letter and returns Kernel32.OpensThroughGlobalDosDevices(letter).
That new internal method in src/FsAnalyser.Core/Native/Kernel32.cs:
- begins a CriticalErrorBoxOff;
- calls the existing private OpenRootForQuery on \\?\GLOBALROOT\GLOBAL??\X:\ (FILE_READ_ATTRIBUTES, FILE_SHARE_ALL, OPEN_EXISTING, FILE_FLAG_BACKUP_SEMANTICS, exactly the spike's open);
- returns !handle.IsInvalid and disposes the handle.
It makes no new call by a Win32 path entry-point name, so FailCriticalErrorsTests' expected list is unchanged. OpenRootForQuery's doc comment is widened to name this third caller. GlobalDosDevices.cs names no entry point of PathOpensGoOnlyThroughTheGuardedOpen, in code or in comments, because that rule matches comments.

StartScan: beside the other Hide* resets, collapse RescanAsAdministratorButton and re-enable it, and clear and collapse RescanStatus.
The Task.Factory.StartNew lambda returns (ScanResult, DriveType?). The DriveType? is RescanAsAdministrator.DriveTypeAnElevatedCopySees(result.Root) when the result is Completed, Unreadable.Count > 0 and !Environment.IsPrivilegedProcess, and null otherwise. It is computed on that same LongRunning task, never on the UI thread.
ShowOutcome takes the DriveType? as a parameter. After the unreadable rows are built, and before ShowVolumeInUse, it sets RescanAsAdministratorButton.Visibility from Offers(result.Status, result.Root, result.Unreadable.Count, driveType, Environment.IsPrivilegedProcess). The RootUnreadable and Refused early returns and both ShowProblem paths leave the button collapsed.
OnRescanAsAdministrator, the Click handler:
- reads LastResult.Root;
- calls RescanAsAdministrator.Start(Environment.ProcessPath, root, Process.Start) on the UI thread;
- on null, disables the button and shows StartedLine;
- on a line, leaves the button enabled and shows that line. Nothing else in the window changes.
Nothing here names the report (existing fitness rule).

